1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,152
A primary key is unique and not null, and there is only one per table.

2
00:00:05,152 --> 00:00:10,277
A UNIQUE constraint is unique, and it may allow nulls depending on the engine.

3
00:00:10,277 --> 00:00:14,319
You use UNIQUE for the candidate keys you did not promote.

4
00:00:14,319 --> 00:00:16,444
email UNIQUE on customers.

5
00:00:16,444 --> 00:00:18,736
ISBN UNIQUE on books.

6
00:00:18,736 --> 00:00:23,722
The primary key is still customer_id or book_id.

7
00:00:23,722 --> 00:00:27,763
Queries and foreign keys prefer the dull primary key.

8
00:00:27,763 --> 00:00:31,027
Humans still need the natural one to stay unique.

9
00:00:31,027 --> 00:00:35,444
SQLite treats NULLs in UNIQUE as distinct from each other.

10
00:00:35,444 --> 00:00:40,611
You can insert two rows with NULL email if email is unique and nullable.

11
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:46,152
PostgreSQL's UNIQUE behaves the same with NULL: NULL does not equal NULL.

12
00:00:46,152 --> 00:00:51,833
If you meant at most one empty email, you wanted a partial unique index or a NOT NULL.

13
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:54,708
Composite unique constraints are common.

14
00:00:54,708 --> 00:00:59,680
UNIQUE student_id, course_id on enrollment.

15
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,833
That is the pair lock from the many-to-many lesson.

16
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:06,430
A unique constraint is an index in most engines.

17
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:08,625
You get a lookup as a side effect.

18
00:01:08,625 --> 00:01:11,694
Do not skip UNIQUE because you plan to be careful.

19
00:01:11,694 --> 00:01:14,138
The second clerk will not be careful.

20
00:01:14,138 --> 00:01:17,361
Naming the constraint makes errors readable.

21
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:23,208
customers_email_key is a better message than a generic unique violation.

22
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:27,375
Primary key versus unique is also about foreign keys.

23
00:01:27,375 --> 00:01:32,986
A foreign key must point at a unique set of columns, usually the primary key.

24
00:01:32,986 --> 00:01:37,305
Pointing at a UNIQUE natural key is allowed in many engines.

25
00:01:37,305 --> 00:01:39,861
It makes later key changes painful.

26
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:41,902
Prefer pointing at the surrogate.

27
00:01:41,902 --> 00:01:45,500
Keep the natural unique for humans and imports.

28
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:53,694
When an import must match on ISBN, look up book_id, then store book_id on the child.

29
00:01:53,694 --> 00:01:59,888
Do not copy ISBN onto every order line unless you are denormalizing on purpose.

30
00:01:59,888 --> 00:02:01,486
One primary key.

31
00:02:01,486 --> 00:02:05,805
As many UNIQUE constraints as you have real candidate keys.

32
00:02:05,805 --> 00:02:07,541
Nulls called out in public.

33
00:02:07,541 --> 00:02:09,180
That is the whole policy.

34
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:16,402
Partial unique indexes in PostgreSQL let you say email is unique among rows that are not deleted.

35
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:19,055
WHERE deleted_at IS NULL.

36
00:02:19,055 --> 00:02:22,597
That is how soft-delete and uniqueness coexist.

37
00:02:22,597 --> 00:02:29,611
Without it, a retired email blocks a new customer forever, or you reuse emails in a mess.

38
00:02:29,611 --> 00:02:34,222
MySQL and SQLite have their own partial-index stories.

39
00:02:34,222 --> 00:02:36,708
Read them before you promise the policy.

40
00:02:36,708 --> 00:02:40,013
Case folding belongs with uniqueness for emails.

41
00:02:40,013 --> 00:02:46,527
A unique index on lower(email) is a functional unique index in PostgreSQL.

42
00:02:46,527 --> 00:02:49,833
Without it, Maya@ and maya@ both get in.

43
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:52,347
Include that in the candidate-key meeting.

44
00:02:52,347 --> 00:02:54,652
Uniqueness is a business rule.

45
00:02:54,652 --> 00:02:58,152
The constraint is how the rule survives a busy hour.

46
00:02:58,152 --> 00:03:03,069
When an import fails on UNIQUE, the failure is data, not drama.

47
00:03:03,069 --> 00:03:04,430
Show the duplicate.

48
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:06,236
Merge or reject.

49
00:03:06,236 --> 00:03:09,236
Do not DROP INDEX to make the import finish.

50
00:03:09,236 --> 00:03:11,013
That is how you buy a weekend.

51
00:03:11,013 --> 00:03:13,750
Primary key names are often implicit.

52
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:15,694
UNIQUE names should not be.

53
00:03:15,694 --> 00:03:17,500
You will read them in logs.

54
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,013
Treat them as prose.

55
00:03:19,013 --> 00:03:24,652
One primary, many UNIQUE, nulls explicit, pointers at the surrogate.

56
00:03:24,652 --> 00:03:26,805
You can run a store on that sentence.

57
00:03:26,805 --> 00:03:31,361
Most uniqueness bugs are a missing sentence, not a missing feature.

58
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:33,486
Write it, then type UNIQUE.

59
00:03:33,486 --> 00:03:36,041
Uniqueness is a product promise.

60
00:03:36,041 --> 00:03:38,791
The database is the enforcer you hired.

61
00:03:38,791 --> 00:03:43,069
If you fire the enforcer for an import, you own the duplicates.

62
00:03:43,069 --> 00:03:44,458
Keep the enforcer.

63
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:45,958
Stage the import.

64
00:03:45,958 --> 00:03:47,388
Merge in daylight.

65
00:03:47,388 --> 00:03:48,902
UNIQUE is daylight.

66
00:03:48,902 --> 00:03:51,486
Null policy is part of the promise.

67
00:03:51,486 --> 00:03:54,652
Say whether unknown emails are allowed twice.

68
00:03:54,652 --> 00:03:56,069
If no, NOT NULL.

69
00:03:56,069 --> 00:04:00,305
If yes among retired rows, a partial unique index.

70
00:04:00,305 --> 00:04:03,597
Write that in the same comment as the constraint name.

71
00:04:03,597 --> 00:04:06,805
Future incidents will open that comment first.

72
00:04:06,805 --> 00:04:08,180
Make it worth opening.

73
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:12,416
One primary, many UNIQUE, pointers at the dull key.

74
00:04:12,416 --> 00:04:13,569
Keep saying it.

75
00:04:13,569 --> 00:04:17,916
Constraints are rules the engine enforces so rows stay honest.

76
00:04:17,916 --> 00:04:23,097
Think of them as traffic rules: not suggestions, not application hope.

77
00:04:23,097 --> 00:04:26,861
PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE both refuse duplicates.

78
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:28,500
They are not the same tool.

79
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:34,861
A primary key identifies the row, is unique, and in SQL is also NOT NULL.

80
00:04:34,861 --> 00:04:37,277
A table has one primary key.

81
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:43,680
Foreign keys point at it, or at a unique column you explicitly allow, depending on the engine.

82
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:49,680
UNIQUE can sit on email, username, or phone while the primary key is a dull id.

83
00:04:49,802 --> 00:04:57,988
UNIQUE may allow NULL in many engines, and NULLs do not collide with each other the way values do, depending on the dialect.

84
00:04:57,988 --> 00:05:06,168
Two users with NULL email can both exist under UNIQUE(email) in engines that treat NULL as distinct.

85
00:05:06,168 --> 00:05:14,696
If email must be present and unique, you want UNIQUE and NOT NULL, or a primary key on email, which carries NOT NULL.

86
00:05:14,696 --> 00:05:18,849
Do not make email the primary key because it is unique today.

87
00:05:18,849 --> 00:05:20,321
People change email.

88
00:05:20,321 --> 00:05:24,460
Children that pointed at the old email become a migration.

89
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:30,654
Dull id as primary key, UNIQUE on email, is the usual adult design.

90
00:05:30,654 --> 00:05:37,252
The extra thing a primary key does is say: this is the identity other tables should copy.

91
00:05:37,252 --> 00:05:43,988
UNIQUE says: this value may not repeat, but it is not necessarily the identity of the row.

92
00:05:43,988 --> 00:05:50,029
Choosing UNIQUE when you meant primary key leaves you without a natural parent for foreign keys.

93
00:05:50,029 --> 00:05:54,502
Choosing primary key on a value you will edit breaks references.

94
00:05:54,502 --> 00:05:57,071
You can have several UNIQUE constraints.

95
00:05:57,071 --> 00:05:59,627
You cannot have several primary keys.

96
00:05:59,627 --> 00:06:07,543
Composite UNIQUE (store_id, sku) means the pair is unique, not each column alone.

97
00:06:07,543 --> 00:06:09,835
Two stores may sell the same sku.

98
00:06:09,835 --> 00:06:13,029
Name the constraints so errors are readable.

99
00:06:13,029 --> 00:06:22,293
When implementing, write PRIMARY KEY on the identity, UNIQUE on natural handles people search, FOREIGN KEY on the copies.

100
00:06:22,293 --> 00:06:26,502
Then duplicates fail at the door instead of in a nightly cleanup.

101
00:06:26,502 --> 00:06:28,377
That failure is the feature.

102
00:06:28,377 --> 00:06:34,377
If an application needs to tell a user that username is taken, catch the unique violation.

103
00:06:34,377 --> 00:06:38,390
Do not SELECT then INSERT and hope nobody else grabbed the name.

104
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:40,877
The constraint is the race-proof rule.

105
00:06:40,877 --> 00:06:46,724
Primary versus unique is the difference between who you are and what else must not collide.

106
00:06:46,724 --> 00:06:48,946
Keep who you are dull and stable.

107
00:06:48,946 --> 00:06:51,960
Keep collisions on the columns humans care about.

108
00:06:51,960 --> 00:07:01,877
Recommended practice: surrogate primary key, unique natural handles, foreign keys to the surrogate, not null on handles that login needs.

109
00:07:01,877 --> 00:07:08,224
Anti-pattern: unique without not null on email, then three accounts with empty email.

110
00:07:08,224 --> 00:07:15,877
Anti-pattern: primary key on phone, then a user changes phone and every child row needs a cascade.

111
00:07:15,877 --> 00:07:25,932
Anti-pattern: no unique on username, primary key on id, application checks uniqueness with SELECT, two signups collide.

112
00:07:25,932 --> 00:07:28,224
The constraint was the missing piece.

113
00:07:28,224 --> 00:07:34,404
Primary versus unique is those practices, not a trivia difference in nullability only.

114
00:07:34,404 --> 00:07:36,738
Nullability is one difference.

115
00:07:36,738 --> 00:07:39,279
Count of keys per table is another.

116
00:07:39,279 --> 00:07:42,835
Who children point at is the one that breaks designs.

117
00:07:42,835 --> 00:07:44,238
Point at identity.

118
00:07:44,238 --> 00:07:46,377
Uniquely constrain the rest.

119
00:07:46,377 --> 00:07:50,015
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

120
00:07:50,015 --> 00:07:53,071
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

121
00:07:53,071 --> 00:07:54,502
What is a constraint?

122
00:07:54,502 --> 00:07:58,196
A rule the engine enforces, not application hope.

123
00:07:58,196 --> 00:08:01,127
What do PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE share?

124
00:08:01,127 --> 00:08:03,404
They refuse duplicate values.

125
00:08:03,404 --> 00:08:05,765
How many primary keys per table?

126
00:08:05,765 --> 00:08:06,557
One.

127
00:08:06,557 --> 00:08:08,710
How many UNIQUE constraints?

128
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:11,071
As many as you have honest handles.

129
00:08:11,071 --> 00:08:13,432
Does PRIMARY KEY allow NULL?

130
00:08:13,432 --> 00:08:14,196
No.

131
00:08:14,196 --> 00:08:15,946
Does UNIQUE allow NULL?

132
00:08:15,946 --> 00:08:20,571
Often yes, and NULLs may not collide, depending on dialect.

133
00:08:20,571 --> 00:08:22,279
What should children point at?

134
00:08:22,279 --> 00:08:25,585
The identity, usually a dull primary key.

135
00:08:25,585 --> 00:08:27,724
Why not primary key on email?

136
00:08:27,724 --> 00:08:30,918
People change email; children would have to move.

137
00:08:30,918 --> 00:08:33,085
Why UNIQUE on email anyway?

138
00:08:33,085 --> 00:08:35,293
Login still cannot share an email.

139
00:08:35,293 --> 00:08:37,071
Composite UNIQUE meaning?

140
00:08:37,071 --> 00:08:40,210
The pair is unique, not each column alone.

141
00:08:40,210 --> 00:08:42,557
SELECT then INSERT for uniqueness?

142
00:08:42,557 --> 00:08:43,613
A race.

143
00:08:43,613 --> 00:08:45,085
Use the constraint.

144
00:08:45,085 --> 00:08:46,446
Traffic metaphor?

145
00:08:46,446 --> 00:08:50,335
Unique is a parking rule; primary key is the VIN.

146
00:08:50,335 --> 00:08:53,571
Can UNIQUE replace a primary key for identity?

147
00:08:53,571 --> 00:08:56,668
It can be unique without being the pointer you want.

148
00:08:56,668 --> 00:08:58,127
Named constraints?

149
00:08:58,127 --> 00:09:00,877
Yes, so errors read as English.

150
00:09:00,877 --> 00:09:02,182
Username taken?

151
00:09:02,182 --> 00:09:04,182
Catch the unique violation.

152
00:09:04,182 --> 00:09:06,057
Phone UNIQUE NULL allowed?

153
00:09:06,057 --> 00:09:08,113
Two NULLs may both exist.

154
00:09:08,113 --> 00:09:10,029
Email UNIQUE NOT NULL?

155
00:09:10,029 --> 00:09:14,377
Present and unique, still not necessarily the primary key.

156
00:09:14,377 --> 00:09:16,599
store_id plus sku?

157
00:09:16,599 --> 00:09:20,432
Composite unique so two stores share sku text.

158
00:09:20,432 --> 00:09:22,960
Who are you versus what must not collide?

159
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,918
Primary versus unique.

160
00:09:24,918 --> 00:09:26,321
Recommended pattern?

161
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:32,321
Surrogate primary key, unique natural handles, foreign keys to the surrogate.

162
00:09:32,321 --> 00:09:37,043
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

